package com.example.web;

import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;
import org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.transaction.TransactionException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ControllerAdvice;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;

@ControllerAdvice
public class DependencyFailureAdvice {
    // RTE-007: database down is 503 (rendered by templates/error/5xx.html), never 404 and never a 200 shell.
    // Broad on purpose; narrow it if SQL bugs should stay 500.
    @ExceptionHandler({DataAccessException.class, TransactionException.class})
    void unavailable(HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        response.setHeader("Retry-After", "30");
        response.sendError(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE.value());
    }
}

Spring Boot 3.4 and newer including 4.x + Spring Security 6/7 + Thymeleaf + spring-boot-starter-validation. A plain HTML form that works without JavaScript: th:action adds the CSRF token (Spring Security's default CSRF protection stays ON), Bean Validation checks the fields, delivery goes to your webhook (FORM_WEBHOOK_URL: CRM, form provider or mail API) with 3s connect and 8s read timeouts, and the thank-you redirect happens only after a 2xx answer; any other answer or a timeout becomes 502 (rendered by templates/error/5xx.html from the error-pages recipe). Spring's "redirect:" prefix answers 302, so the 303 is requested through View.RESPONSE_STATUS_ATTRIBUTE. The in-memory limiter is per instance and keyed on getRemoteAddr(): behind a proxy set server.forward-headers-strategy=native so it sees the client address, and use Redis (or Bucket4j with a distributed backend) when you run several instances. Add Cloudflare Turnstile (cf-turnstile-response, verified server-side) if bots get past the honeypot.
