Spring Boot 3.4 and newer including 4.x (Spring Security 6.4 to 7; compiled and run on Boot 3.5.16 and 4.1.1; on Boot 4 the web starter is spring-boot-starter-webmvc; `permissionsPolicyHeader` is the non-deprecated name, `permissionsPolicy` is deprecated for removal) with Thymeleaf. Spring Security has no CSP nonce feature, so a small filter makes the nonce and writes the whole Content-Security-Policy; the filter is NOT a @Component, so Boot does not also register it as a plain servlet filter (a redundant second registration). Spring Security already sends X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY, HSTS (only on requests it sees as HTTPS, so behind a TLS-terminating proxy set server.forward-headers-strategy=native) and `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, max-age=0, must-revalidate` on HTML; it steps aside when a response already has its own Cache-Control, so the asset rule below wins. Boot's own `spring.web.resources.cache.cachecontrol.*` has no `immutable` option, so hashed assets get a resource handler with CacheControl.immutable() (Framework 6.0.5+).
